Q2 — Animate Multiple CSS Properties
The journal's second program animates multiple CSS properties:
width
height
opacity
left

    BDNS Journal
Step 1 — Create q2.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>

<head>

    <title>Basic jQuery</title>

    <script src="jquery-3.6.1.min.js"></script>

    <script>

        $(document).ready(function () {

            $("#b1").click(function () {

                $("#div1").animate({
                    width: "150px",
                    height: "150px",
                    opacity: "0.5",
                    left: "150"
                });

            });

        });

    </script>

    <style>

        #div1 {
            width: 100px;
            height: 100px;
            background: yellow;
            position: relative;
        }

    </style>

</head>

<body>

    <p>Animation</p>

    <div id="div1"></div>

    <button id="b1">Click to Animate</button>

</body>

</html>

Step 2 — Open q2.html
Initially you should see:
Animation

[small square]

[Click to Animate]

The square initially has:
Width  = 100px
Height = 100px

Step 3 — Click Click to Animate
The square will:
- Increase width
- Increase height
- Become partially transparent
- Move to the right
Output
The journal shows the square becoming larger and moving after the button is clicked.     BDNS Journal
Q3 — Method Chaining
The journal's third question demonstrates jQuery method chaining.     BDNS Journal
The important line is:
$("#p1").css("color","blue").slideUp(2000).slideDown(2000)

This performs multiple jQuery methods on the same element.
Step 1 — Create q3.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>

<head>

    <title>Basic jQuery</title>

    <script src="jquery-3.3.1.min.js"></script>

    <script>

        $(document).ready(function () {

            $("button").click(function () {

                $("#p1")
                    .css("color", "blue")
                    .slideUp(2000)
                    .slideDown(2000);

            });

        });

    </script>

</head>

<body>

    <p id="p1">Romal:239720</p>

    <button>Click Me</button>

</body>

</html>

This follows the journal's method-chaining example.     BDNS Journal
